@node Controlling Echo
@subsection Choose Typing Echo

@cindex line echo

@cindex character echo

@cindex word echo

Character echo speaks each character you type.  Word echo speaks a word when
you finish it@comma{} and optional line echo speaks a completed line.  Toggle each
kind independently to suit the buffer: character feedback may help in a
form where accuracy matters@comma{} while word echo is often more fluent for prose.

The commands below affect the current buffer.  Press @kbd{C-u} first to
change the corresponding default and apply it to the current buffer.

@table @kbd
@item C-e d k
@findex emacsvox-toggle-character-echo

@kindex C-e d k

@code{emacsvox-toggle-character-echo}

Toggle character echo.
@item C-e d w
@findex emacsvox-toggle-word-echo

@kindex C-e d w

@code{emacsvox-toggle-word-echo}

Toggle word echo.
@item C-e d l
@findex emacsvox-toggle-line-echo

@kindex C-e d l

@code{emacsvox-toggle-line-echo}

Toggle line echo.
@end table

@node Speech Output Control
@subsection Adjust Rate And Spoken Detail

Use these controls to match speech to the current material.  A faster rate may
suit familiar prose; full punctuation can help when inspecting code or exact
text.  Start with rate and punctuation@comma{} then change capitalization or
indentation feedback only when the distinction would help your current task.

The commands affect the current buffer unless their interactive help describes
a global prefix form.  Use @kbd{C-h k} on a key before changing the global
default.

@table @kbd
@item C-e d r
Enter a speech rate for the current buffer with @code{tts-set-rate}.
@item C-e d DIGIT
Choose one of ten predefined rates with @kbd{0} through @kbd{9}.
@item C-e d p
Choose @code{some}@comma{} @code{all}@comma{} or @code{none} punctuation with @code{tts-set-punctuations}.
@item C-e d s
Toggle splitting of mixed-capital identifiers into separate words.
@item C-e d c / C-e d C
Toggle capitalization indication with the lowercase key.  Use the uppercase
key to preview and choose whether capitals use speech@comma{} a tone@comma{} both@comma{} a custom
rule@comma{} or no added indication.
@item C-e d i / C-e d I
Toggle indentation feedback with the lowercase key.  Use the uppercase key
to preview spoken-column and tonal choices.
@end table

Character-rate scaling@comma{} text chunking@comma{} engine-specific controls@comma{} and reusable
presentation profiles are intentionally outside this starter chapter.  Use
live help for the current speech keymap and see @ref{Aural Presentation} when
you want a complete presentation setup rather than a temporary buffer change.

@node Miscellaneous
@subsection Stop Or Restart Speech

Stopping speech ends the current output without disabling future feedback.
Quiet mode is useful when you temporarily want to keep working without speech.
Restart the server after correcting a backend problem or changing the selected
synthesizer.

@table @kbd
@item C-e s
Stop speech now with @code{tts-stop}.
@item C-e d q
Toggle quiet mode in the current buffer.  Press @kbd{C-u} first to toggle
it globally.
@item C-e C-s
Stop and restart the selected speech server.
@item C-e d V
Speak the selected adapter and its version information.
@end table

Use @kbd{C-h m} or the generated keymap reference for server selection@comma{}
repeated-pattern cleanup@comma{} text splitting@comma{} language selection@comma{} and direct engine
controls.  Those specialist settings are intentionally outside this starter
guide.

@node Voice-lock
@section Hear Structure With Voice Lock

Voice Lock is enabled automatically in supported buffers.  It maps faces and
other structural text properties to speech personalities@comma{} allowing comments@comma{}
strings@comma{} headings@comma{} links@comma{} and other elements to sound distinct without adding
words to the document.

Try it in source code@comma{} Org@comma{} Info@comma{} or EWW@.  Move between two differently styled
elements@comma{} such as a comment and a string or a heading and a link.  Press
@kbd{C-e d v} to turn Voice Lock off in that buffer@comma{} compare the same text@comma{}
then press the key again to restore it.

Use @kbd{C-e d C-v} when you want to change Voice Lock globally.  Turning it
off removes this layer of voice changes; it does not silence ordinary speech@comma{}
semantic feedback@comma{} tones@comma{} or auditory icons.

The exact personalities depend on the speech engine and active presentation
settings.  For an explanation of the separate Voice Lock@comma{} face-presentation@comma{}
and semantic-presentation layers@comma{} see @ref{Voice Lock}.  Use
@ref{Aural Presentation} when you want to choose voices@comma{} sounds@comma{} profiles@comma{} or
other reusable presentation behavior.

