@node Speech Backends
@chapter Speech Backends

Emacsvox needs a speech-server process.  For a new installation@comma{} use
@strong{Omnivox}.  Omnivox is the server that Emacsvox talks to; the engine
that produces speech underneath it is platform-specific.  The normal engine
is eSpeak NG on GNU/Linux@comma{} AVSpeechSynthesizer on macOS@comma{} and WinRT
SpeechSynthesizer on Windows.

Omnivox 1.5.1 is the minimum supported release for Emacsvox 2026.9.0.  A
newer compatible Omnivox release may be used@comma{} but the binary examples and
guided WSL2 installer below pin the tested 1.5.1 artifacts exactly.

For a first installation@comma{} follow the linear procedure in
@ref{Installation}.  Return here when you need platform-specific build
details@comma{} compatibility servers@comma{} or deeper diagnostics.  Omnivox build
internals@comma{} engine behavior@comma{} and versioned protocols are documented and
maintained in
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox, the Omnivox repository}.

@menu
* Supported Platform Routes::    Choose the maintained route for this machine.
* Install Omnivox From A Release Binary: Installing An Omnivox Release Binary.  Install the verified Omnivox 1.5.1 archive.
* Install An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2.  Use the matching verified Windows archive without a build toolchain.
* Trying Native Windows Emacsvox:: Evaluate native Windows Emacs and Omnivox with bounded checks.
* Installing Omnivox From Source:: Build when a release archive does not match the host.
* Building Omnivox For WSL2::    Stage the Windows runtime from WSL2.
* Selecting And Checking Omnivox:: Prove speech before starting Emacsvox.
* Compatibility Speech Servers:: Understand the retained direct adapters.
* Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership.  Locate failures and authoritative detail.
@end menu

@node Supported Platform Routes
@section Supported Platform Routes

The following table describes Emacsvox support@comma{} not every platform that the
Omnivox project can build.  ``Host check required'' means that automated build
and archive verification does not prove playback through the user's audio
device; an audible @code{omnivox --check} on that machine remains the acceptance
test.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .31 .51
@headitem Emacs platform
@tab Recommended route
@tab Current support evidence
@item GNU/Linux x86-64 with an Ubuntu 24.04-compatible runtime
