The release-archive routes below are the shortest installation on matching
GNU/Linux@comma{} macOS@comma{} and WSL2 hosts.  A bare executable@comma{} a repository tag@comma{} or an
unpackaged CI artifact is not a complete installation: keep the release's
runtime data and notices beside the executable.

Do not set @code{OMNIVOX_ENGINE} during initial setup.  Its empty value selects
the normal platform engine above.  Engine routing and optional Piper@comma{}
Eloquence@comma{} or DECtalk support are later customizations@comma{} not prerequisites for
first speech.

@node Installing An Omnivox Release Binary
@section Install Omnivox From A Release Binary

Use this route for GNU/Linux x86-64 with an Ubuntu 24.04-compatible runtime@comma{}
macOS on Apple silicon@comma{} or macOS on Intel.  The
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/tag/v1.5.1, Omnivox 1.5.1 release}
provides these archive names:

@multitable @columnfractions .28 .27 .45
@headitem Host
@tab Set @code{platform} to
@tab Archive
@item GNU/Linux x86-64
@tab @code{linux-x64}
@tab @code{omnivox-1.5.1-linux-x64.tar.gz}
@item macOS Apple silicon
@tab @code{macos-arm64}
@tab @code{omnivox-1.5.1-macos-arm64.tar.gz}
@item macOS Intel
@tab @code{macos-x64}
@tab @code{omnivox-1.5.1-macos-x64.tar.gz}
@end multitable

Run @code{uname -m} if the Mac architecture is uncertain: @code{arm64} identifies Apple
silicon and @code{x86_64} identifies Intel.  Create a download directory@comma{} select the
matching platform value@comma{} and fetch both the archive and published checksum
file:

@example
mkdir -p "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.5.1"
cd "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.5.1"
omnivox_version=1.5.1
platform=linux-x64
archive="omnivox-$@{omnivox_version@}-$@{platform@}.tar.gz"
release_url="https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v$@{omnivox_version@}"
curl -fLO "$release_url/$archive"
curl -fLO "$release_url/sha256sums.txt"
@end example

Replace @code{linux-x64} with the matching macOS value from the table.  Verify the
selected archive before extracting it.  On GNU/Linux@comma{} run:

@example
grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | sha256sum --check -
@end example

On macOS@comma{} use its installed @code{shasum} command instead:

@example
grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | shasum -a 256 --check
@end example

The command must name the archive and report @code{OK}.  If it does not@comma{} remove the
download and do not run it.  Extract the verified payload into a versioned
directory and keep every supplied directory beside the executable:

@example
install_dir="$HOME/.local/opt/omnivox-$@{omnivox_version@}"
mkdir -p "$install_dir"
tar -xzf "$archive" -C "$install_dir"
chmod 755 "$install_dir/omnivox"
export PATH="$install_dir:$PATH"
@end example

Add the same @code{PATH} entry to the shell startup file used to launch Emacs so it
remains available in later sessions.  The extracted directory contains
@code{omnivox}@comma{} @code{espeak-ng-data}@comma{} @code{third-party-licenses}@comma{} and
@code{omnivox-voices.el}.  Emacsvox uses its own bundled Lisp adapter@comma{} so do not
load or copy the release's @code{omnivox-voices.el} into Emacsvox.

Now prove the installed binary before starting Emacsvox:

@example
omnivox --version
omnivox --check
omnivox --list-voices
@end example

The version must report 1.5.1 or newer@comma{} and the check must produce both its
tone and spoken confirmation.  The macOS archives are not code-signed or notarized; if
local security policy refuses the verified binary@comma{} use the source route below
rather than weakening that policy.  On GNU/Linux@comma{} a missing runtime library or
incompatible glibc also means that the source route is the appropriate path.

For Emacs running under WSL2@comma{} continue to
@ref{Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2}.  That route
uses the verified Windows archive without requiring a compiler or Docker.

@node Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2
@section Install An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2

This is the recommended WSL2 route for ordinary use on x86-64 Windows and
Windows on ARM@.  From the Emacsvox checkout@comma{} inspect the planned installation
without changing anything:

@example
./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check
@end example

The doctor detects the Windows architecture@comma{} chooses the matching Omnivox
1.5.1 archive@comma{} and reports the Emacs and per-user paths it will use.  If Linux
packages are missing@comma{} it prints one @code{apt} command for you to review and run.
Working @code{powershell.exe} and @code{wslpath} interoperation are required.  After
resolving any reported prerequisite@comma{} run:

@example
./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install
@end example

The installer downloads and checksum-verifies the appropriate Windows archive@comma{}
installs the complete payload below the Windows user's @code{LocalAppData}@comma{} and
records its absolute WSL path in
@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox-program}@comma{} normally
@code{~/.config/emacsvox/omnivox-program}.  It does not invoke @code{sudo} or edit a shell
startup file.  It also compiles Emacsvox@comma{} runs the non-audible launcher
diagnostic@comma{} and finishes with the audible speech-server check.  The normal
acceptance condition is hearing both its tone and speech.

This binary route does not need the Omnivox source checkout@comma{} Docker@comma{} Rust@comma{}
MinGW@comma{} Visual Studio@comma{} Windows .NET Framework@comma{} or an optional/proprietary speech
runtime.  WinRT speech is included in Windows.  Use
@code{./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --no-audio-check} only for a controlled or headless
installation where a separate audible check will be performed later.

@subsection Manual Release Installation

The following procedure is the manual equivalent.  Use it when reviewing the
installer's actions@comma{} working around a local restriction@comma{} or repairing one
layer independently.  It needs @code{curl}@comma{} @code{unzip}@comma{} @code{sha256sum}@comma{} and working
@code{powershell.exe} and @code{wslpath} interoperation.  The commands show Windows x64;
on Windows on ARM@comma{} use @code{windows-arm64} in both the archive and versioned install
directory and verify that archive's published checksum.

Download the Windows x64 archive and the release checksum file in WSL:

@example
mkdir -p "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.5.1"
cd "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.5.1"
omnivox_version=1.5.1
archive="omnivox-$@{omnivox_version@}-windows-x64.zip"
release_url="https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v$@{omnivox_version@}"
curl -fLO "$release_url/$archive"
curl -fLO "$release_url/sha256sums.txt"
grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | sha256sum --check -
@end example

The final command must name the archive and report @code{OK}.  If it does not@comma{}
remove the download and do not run it.  Install the complete archive in a
versioned directory on the Windows filesystem:

@example
windows_local_app_data="$(powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive \
  -Command '[Environment]::GetFolderPath("LocalApplicationData")' | tr -d '\r')"
install_dir="$(wslpath -u "$windows_local_app_data")/Emacsvox/Omnivox/releases/$omnivox_version-windows-x64"
mkdir -p "$install_dir"
unzip -q -o "$archive" -d "$install_dir"
chmod 755 "$install_dir/omnivox.exe"
config_home=$@{XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-"$HOME/.config"@}
mkdir -p "$config_home/emacsvox"
printf '%s\n' "$install_dir/omnivox.exe" > \
  "$config_home/emacsvox/omnivox-program"
@end example

Keep @code{omnivox.exe}@comma{} @code{espeak-ng-data}@comma{} and @code{third-party-licenses} together in
that directory.  Do not copy or load the archive's @code{omnivox-voices.el};
Emacsvox uses its bundled adapter.  The data-only configuration file makes the
selection persistent without changing shell initialization.  Use
@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME} instead of @code{~/.config} when it is set.  An explicit
@code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} remains a temporary override and takes precedence over this
file.

From the Emacsvox checkout@comma{} verify the selected release and listen to its
physical audio check:

@example
./servers/omnivox --version
./servers/omnivox --list-voices
./servers/omnivox --check
@end example

The version must report 1.5.1 or newer.  The check must produce both its tone
and spoken confirmation.  Release CI verifies the downloaded archive@comma{} Windows x64
architecture@comma{} adjacent eSpeak data@comma{} voice discovery@comma{} and headless synthesis
through both WinRT and eSpeak@comma{} but only this audible check proves the local
audio path.  The Windows archive is not code-signed.  If Windows security or
local policy refuses it@comma{} do not weaken that policy to complete the install.

The generic Omnivox 1.5.1 archive does not contain optional Piper@comma{} Eloquence@comma{}
DECtalk@comma{} RHVoice@comma{} Flite@comma{} or RuTTS helpers or runtimes.  They are not needed for
normal WinRT speech.  Omnivox 1.5.1 publishes a separate matching Piper
companion@comma{} while Emacsvox's clean source-build route below verifies and stages
that companion as part of its content-addressed Windows bundle.  The
development route builds the newer RHVoice@comma{} Flite@comma{} and RuTTS integrations while
deliberately omitting that older Piper companion.  A Piper voice model and the
RHVoice runtime and voices remain separate user-supplied files.  Flite and
RuTTS need no external runtime installation in the source-built bundle.

@node Trying Native Windows Emacsvox
@section Trying Native Windows Emacsvox

This is a @emph{preview evaluation}@comma{} not yet the recommended first installation.
It runs both Emacs and Omnivox directly on Windows; it does not use WSL2 or the
POSIX @code{bin/emacsvox} and @code{servers/omnivox} launchers.  The procedure is
self-contained so a useful result records exactly where success or failure
occurred.

You need a native Windows build of Emacs 31 or newer@comma{} PowerShell@comma{} Git@comma{} an
Emacsvox checkout on the Windows filesystem@comma{} and network access for the
Omnivox release download.  Windows audio must work and at least one Windows
speech voice must be installed.  You do not need WSL2@comma{} Docker@comma{} Rust@comma{} MinGW@comma{}
Visual Studio@comma{} or Windows .NET Framework.  Use @code{windows-x64} on an Intel or
AMD Windows computer.  Use @code{windows-arm64} only on Windows on ARM@.

If Emacsvox is not already checked out on Windows@comma{} open PowerShell and place a
checkout on the Windows filesystem:

@example
$SourceRoot = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE "src"
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $SourceRoot | Out-Null
Set-Location $SourceRoot
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox.git
$Emacsvox = Join-Path $SourceRoot "emacsvox"
@end example

If the checkout already exists@comma{} skip @code{git clone} and set @code{$Emacsvox} to its
absolute Windows path instead.  Continue in the same PowerShell window.

@subsection Download And Verify Omnivox

Set @code{$Platform} for the computer.  The example below uses the usual Intel or
AMD Windows x64 archive:

@example
$Version = "1.5.1"
$Platform = "windows-x64" # Use windows-arm64 only on Windows on ARM.
$DownloadDir = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE "Downloads\omnivox-$Version"
$Archive = "omnivox-$Version-$Platform.zip"
$ReleaseUrl = "https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v$Version"

New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $DownloadDir | Out-Null
$ArchivePath = Join-Path $DownloadDir $Archive
$ChecksumPath = Join-Path $DownloadDir "sha256sums.txt"
Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing "$ReleaseUrl/$Archive" -OutFile $ArchivePath
Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing "$ReleaseUrl/sha256sums.txt" -OutFile $ChecksumPath

$Pattern = '\s+\*?' + [regex]::Escape($Archive) + '$'
$ChecksumLine = Get-Content $ChecksumPath |
  Where-Object @{ $_ -match $Pattern @} |
  Select-Object -First 1
if (-not $ChecksumLine) @{ throw "No published checksum for $Archive" @}
$Expected = ($ChecksumLine -split '\s+')[0].ToLowerInvariant()
$Actual = (Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 $ArchivePath).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()
if ($Actual -ne $Expected) @{ throw "Checksum mismatch for $Archive" @}
Write-Host "Checksum OK: $Archive"
@end example

Do not extract or run the archive unless the last line reports @code{Checksum OK}.
The Windows release is not code-signed.  If Windows security or local policy
refuses it@comma{} stop rather than weakening that policy.

@subsection Install And Check The Native Server

Continue in the same PowerShell window.  Extract the complete release into a
versioned directory below the current user's @code{LocalAppData}:

@example
$InstallDir = Join-Path $env:LOCALAPPDATA "Emacsvox\Omnivox\releases\$Version-$Platform"
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $InstallDir | Out-Null
Expand-Archive -LiteralPath $ArchivePath -DestinationPath $InstallDir -Force
$Omnivox = Join-Path $InstallDir "omnivox.exe"

foreach ($Required in @@(
  $Omnivox@comma{}
  (Join-Path $InstallDir "espeak-ng-data\phontab")@comma{}
  (Join-Path $InstallDir "third-party-licenses\THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md")
)) @{
  if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $Required -PathType Leaf)) @{
    throw "Incomplete Omnivox installation: $Required"
  @}
@}

& $Omnivox --version
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) @{ throw "Omnivox version check failed" @}
& $Omnivox --list-voices
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) @{ throw "Omnivox voice discovery failed" @}
& $Omnivox --check
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) @{ throw "Omnivox audio check failed" @}
@end example

The version must report 1.5.1 or newer@comma{} voice discovery must list at least one
Windows voice@comma{} and the final check must produce both a tone and spoken confirmation.
Keep @code{omnivox.exe}@comma{} @code{espeak-ng-data}@comma{} and @code{third-party-licenses} together.
Do not copy or load the archive's @code{omnivox-voices.el}: it is the adapter for
upstream Emacspeak@comma{} while Emacsvox supplies its own adapter.

@subsection Start Native Emacsvox

Keep the same PowerShell window so @code{$Omnivox} still identifies the verified
executable.  Set @code{$Emacs} to the native @code{emacs.exe}.  If this window did not
create the Emacsvox checkout above@comma{} also set @code{$Emacsvox} to that checkout:

@example
$Emacs = "C:\path\to\emacs\bin\emacs.exe"
# If not set above@comma{} uncomment and edit the next line:
# $Emacsvox = "C:\path\to\emacsvox"

& $Emacs --version
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) @{ throw "Could not run native Emacs" @}
$env:TTS_PROGRAM = $Omnivox
& $Emacs -Q --no-splash --load "$Emacsvox\lisp\emacsvox-setup.el"
@end example

The Emacs version must be 31 or newer.  Use @code{emacs.exe}@comma{} rather than
@code{runemacs.exe}@comma{} for this first trial so startup errors remain visible in the
PowerShell window.  Loading @code{emacsvox-setup.el} directly is intentional: the
repository's @code{bin/emacsvox} launcher is a POSIX shell script and is not the
native Windows entry point.

@subsection Accept Or Report The Trial

A successful trial satisfies every item below:

@enumerate
@item
Emacsvox speaks its startup introduction.

@item
@kbd{C-h v tts-program RET} displays the absolute path to the
verified @code{omnivox.exe}.

@item
@kbd{M-x tts-speak-version RET} speaks the Omnivox version
response@comma{} and @kbd{M-x omnivox-list-voices RET} displays the
Windows voices.

@item
@kbd{C-n} and @kbd{C-p} speak ordinary line
movement@comma{} @kbd{C-e s} stops current speech@comma{} and
@kbd{M-x tts-restart RET} restores speech without a process
error.

@item
Type @kbd{M-x emacsvox-toggle-icons RET} twice.  The second
toggle@comma{} which turns icons back on@comma{} plays a short auditory icon in addition
to plain speech.
@end enumerate

If the direct @code{--check} fails@comma{} diagnose Omnivox or Windows audio before
starting Emacs.  If it succeeds but Emacsvox reports that it cannot find the
speech server@comma{} confirm in the same PowerShell window that
@code{$env:TTS_PROGRAM} contains the absolute executable path and that
@code{Test-Path $env:TTS_PROGRAM} returns @code{True}.  If the process starts and then
exits@comma{} retain the PowerShell error@comma{} the Emacs @code{*Messages*} buffer@comma{} Windows
version and architecture@comma{} the complete @code{emacs.exe --version} output@comma{} and
@code{omnivox.exe --version} output.

After a successful trial@comma{} the same absolute selection can be placed before
loading Emacsvox in the user's Emacs initialization file:

@lisp
(setenv "TTS_PROGRAM"
        "C:/Users/NAME/AppData/Local/Emacsvox/Omnivox/releases/1.5.1-windows-x64/omnivox.exe")
(load-file "C:/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")
@end lisp

Replace both example paths.  This initial configuration deliberately uses an
absolute executable path.  Adding the Omnivox directory to @code{exec-path} is
optional and is needed only when later selecting it by the short name
@code{omnivox}.  A successful report is evidence for promoting native Windows out
of preview; it does not silently change the support status on its own.

@node Installing Omnivox From Source
@section Installing Omnivox From Source

Use this route on GNU/Linux and macOS@.  It requires network access for the
initial clone@comma{} Rust toolchain@comma{} and locked dependency downloads.

Install these prerequisite classes before building:

@itemize
@item
@uref{https://rustup.rs/, rustup}; the checked-in
@code{rust-toolchain.toml} selects the supported Rust release.

@item
Python 3@comma{} a C and C++ toolchain@comma{} CMake@comma{} and Clang with libclang.  The supported
build wrapper uses these to build and stage eSpeak NG and its generated data.

@item
On GNU/Linux@comma{} @code{pkg-config} and ALSA development headers.  The ALSA
package is commonly named @code{libasound2-dev} on Debian or Ubuntu and
@code{alsa-lib-devel} on Fedora.

@item
On macOS@comma{} the Xcode command-line tools.  Install them with
@code{xcode-select --install} if they are absent.
@end itemize

Keep the two repositories beside one another; this also matches the WSL2
release layout:

@example
cd /path/containing/emacsvox
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox
cd omnivox
make build
make install
@end example

@code{make build} performs the locked release build and stages the matching
eSpeak data and third-party notices.  @code{make install} installs the
binary and those adjacent directories@comma{} normally below @code{~/.cargo/bin}.
Set @code{OMNIVOX_INSTALL_BIN} before @code{make install} only when the Cargo
binary directory is elsewhere.  Do not use a bare @code{cargo build} as the
installation procedure; it does not create the complete staged payload.

Make the install directory visible on @code{PATH} before continuing.  No
proprietary speech runtime is needed for the recommended Linux or macOS path.

@node Building Omnivox For WSL2
@section Building Omnivox For WSL2

Use this advanced route when maintaining the reproducible Emacsvox Windows
bundle or its optional engine integrations.  Ordinary WSL2 users should use
the pre-built release in
@ref{Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2}.  This target
builds from source and currently produces an x86-64 Windows runtime.

Before running it@comma{} install or configure all of the following:

@itemize
@item
A WSL2 distribution with Windows interoperation enabled.  Both
@code{powershell.exe} and @code{wslpath} must run directly from the WSL shell@comma{} and the
Windows user's @code{LocalAppData} directory must be writable.

@item
A working Docker client and daemon with Linux/AMD64 container support.  The
current WSL user must be able to run @code{docker} without adding @code{sudo} because
the Make target invokes it directly.  Docker Desktop with WSL integration is
one way to provide this@comma{} but it is not the only possible Docker setup.

@item
Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or newer.  This means the Windows-only
``.NET Framework''@comma{} not merely a modern @code{dotnet} SDK@.  Visual Studio is not
required.

@item
In WSL@comma{} @code{git}@comma{} GNU @code{make}@comma{} @code{curl}@comma{} @code{unzip}@comma{} @code{sha256sum}@comma{} and the normal GNU
shell utilities supplied by a standard distribution.  The build also uses
commands such as @code{awk}@comma{} @code{cmp}@comma{} @code{find}@comma{} @code{readlink}@comma{} @code{sed}@comma{} @code{sort}@comma{} and
@code{xargs}.

@item
Network access for the first Docker image build@comma{} Rust dependency download@comma{}
and two checksum-pinned NuGet packages.  Later builds reuse the local Docker@comma{}
Cargo@comma{} and .NET package caches.

@item
Emacsvox and Omnivox Git checkouts.  By default they must be sibling
directories named @code{emacsvox} and @code{omnivox}@comma{} although maintainers can set
@code{OMNIVOX_DIR} to an alternate Omnivox checkout.  The Omnivox checkout must
be at the exact source commit recorded for the checksum-pinned Piper
companion in @code{servers/omnivox-release/toolchain.lock} when using the clean
release target.  The preparation target rejects a different revision instead
of combining unmatched native components.  The development target described
below accepts current source by omitting Piper.  Both checkouts need enough
free space for the Docker image@comma{} native and cross-compiled build products@comma{}
and caches.
@end itemize

You do @emph{not} need to install Rust@comma{} MinGW@comma{} CMake@comma{} Clang@comma{} libclang@comma{} eSpeak NG@comma{}
Roslyn@comma{} Visual Studio@comma{} or the .NET reference assemblies in WSL@.  The target
pins or builds those inside its controlled toolchain.  Eloquence and DECtalk
runtimes and the RHVoice runtime and voice data are optional and are not needed
for the normal Windows WinRT voice.

The quickest prerequisite check is:

@example
cd /path/to/emacsvox
make verify-windows-omnivox-toolchain
@end example

This is more than a presence check: on its first run it downloads the pinned
.NET compiler packages@comma{} builds the pinned Docker image@comma{} and verifies the exact
Rust@comma{} MinGW@comma{} libclang@comma{} Roslyn@comma{} and .NET Framework versions.  A failure names the
missing host command or unsupported framework before the full Omnivox build is
attempted.

Clone Omnivox beside Emacsvox as shown in the source-build section.  Then@comma{} from
a clean Emacsvox checkout whose sibling Omnivox checkout also has no tracked
changes@comma{} run:

@example
cd /path/to/emacsvox
make windows-omnivox
@end example

This is the reproducible release path.  It builds and verifies a pinned
Windows-GNU executable and stages a content-addressed runtime beneath
@code{servers/omnivox-bin}.  Once the Piper lock identifies an Omnivox revision that
contains these integrations@comma{} the payload includes the matching@comma{}
checksum-pinned Piper companion@comma{} the RHVoice helper without an RHVoice runtime@comma{}
the Flite helper with its compiled CMU US SLT voice@comma{} and the RuTTS helper with
its built-in male and female Russian voices.  It contains no Piper model and no
RuLex component.  It also copies the runnable payload to a Windows-local
directory so native voice and eSpeak data discovery do not pay the WSL
filesystem cost.  Optional Eloquence@comma{} DECtalk@comma{} and RHVoice runtimes are not
downloaded by this target or required for normal WinRT speech.

The build does not report success on file checks alone.  Its final live gate
starts the exact Windows-local runtime@comma{} discovers Flite's SLT voice and both
RuTTS voices@comma{} and requires each helper to synthesize a non-empty WAV@.  Repeat
that gate without rebuilding by running @code{make verify-windows-omnivox-live}.

To register Piper@comma{} review a model and its model card@comma{} keep its adjacent
@code{.onnx.json} or @code{.json} configuration with it@comma{} and supply an absolute path
while staging:

@example
OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL=/absolute/path/to/voice.onnx make windows-omnivox
@end example

The target copies the model and configuration into a separate@comma{}
content-addressed directory below Windows @code{LocalAppData} and records their
hashes without bundling them into the runtime.  Omitting
@code{OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL} installs the companion but leaves Piper unregistered.
After a configured build@comma{} start a fresh speech process and confirm that the
physical voice is present:

@example
./servers/omnivox --engine piper --list-voices
@end example

In Emacsvox@comma{} restart both speech processes or restart Emacs@comma{} then press
@code{C-e d e}.  That command lists the engines reported by the live process@comma{} so
Piper appears only after the newly staged server has started and successfully
loaded its model and helper.

Maintainers testing tracked changes should use the development route in
@ref{Integration Maintenance} rather than weakening the clean-release
guard:

@example
OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_LIBRARY=/mnt/c/path/to/RHVoice.dll \
  OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_DATA=/mnt/c/path/to/RHVoice/data \
  make windows-omnivox-dev
./servers/omnivox --engine flite --list-voices
./servers/omnivox --engine rutts --list-voices
@end example

This route builds current Omnivox source@comma{} stages the RHVoice helper@comma{} Flite with
its SLT voice@comma{} and RuTTS 6.3.3 with its built-in male and female Russian voices@comma{}
and deliberately omits the older pinned Piper companion.  RuTTS needs no
separate installation; RuLex is not included.  RHVoice appears only after
installing a compatible Windows @code{RHVoice.dll} plus language and voice data.
When its library and data variables are supplied to the development target@comma{}
staging validates them and records Windows path pointers beside that local
runtime; the launcher restores them for later fresh Emacsvox processes.  Also
supply @code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_CONFIG} when the installation has a separate
configuration directory.  The library@comma{} data@comma{} and configuration remain external
and are not copied into the Omnivox payload.  Installation and validation
instructions are in Omnivox's checkout under @code{docs/RHVOICE.md} and
@code{docs/RUTTS.md}.  Restart both speech processes or Emacs after restaging or
changing those inputs@comma{} then use @code{C-e d e} to inspect the engines reported by the
new process.

@node Selecting And Checking Omnivox
@section Selecting And Checking Omnivox

First test Omnivox without Emacs.  On GNU/Linux or macOS@comma{} run the installed
binary:

@example
omnivox --version
omnivox --check
omnivox --list-voices
@end example

Under WSL2@comma{} test the Emacsvox launcher.  It selects an explicit
@code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} first@comma{} then the per-user installation recorded by the guided
installer@comma{} then a content-addressed source-built runtime@comma{} and finally an
@code{omnivox} command on @code{PATH}:

@example
./servers/omnivox --check
@end example

The check initializes the selected engine and audio output@comma{} then plays a tone
and speech.  Success ends with a message saying that everything is working if
both were heard.  Do not continue merely because the command exited zero:
hearing both signals is the backend acceptance condition.

Next select the server before loading Emacsvox:

@example
export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox
@end example

Make that setting visible in the shell used to launch Emacs.  An
@code{export} affects only that shell and its children; placing it in a
shell startup file makes the server choice persistent for later sessions.  On
POSIX systems@comma{} Emacsvox's @code{servers/omnivox} launcher also finds an installed
@code{omnivox} on @code{PATH}.  @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} can name an absolute executable as an
explicit override.  Under WSL2@comma{} the guided installer's data-only configuration
file is persistent across launching shells and takes precedence over a
source-built staged runtime.
Native Windows Emacs resolves @code{omnivox.exe} directly through @code{exec-path}@comma{} or
uses the absolute executable named by @code{TTS_PROGRAM}.  That route remains
preview.  For its bounded evaluation procedure@comma{} see
@ref{Trying Native Windows Emacsvox}.

Inside a running Emacsvox session@comma{} @code{M-x omnivox} selects the same server
temporarily.  Use @code{M-x tts-speak-version} to identify the live adapter
and @code{M-x tts-restart} after changing server configuration.  This backend
contract stops at a proven speech server; the complete isolated Emacs start@comma{}
expected startup speech@comma{} and no-speech recovery belong to the first-use
procedure.

@node Compatibility Speech Servers
@section Compatibility Speech Servers

Emacsvox retains direct speech servers for existing installations.  They are
not the recommended first setup and are outside the initial-user platform
matrix above:

@table @code
@item espeak
The direct GNU/Linux Tcl server.  Building it with @code{make espeak}
requires Tclx at runtime@comma{} Tcl 8.6 development headers@comma{} eSpeak NG development
libraries@comma{} and a C++ compiler.  It remains useful for compatibility testing@comma{}
but new users should install Omnivox instead.
@item swiftmac
The direct Swift macOS server.  It requires Swift 5.9 or newer and is built
with @code{make swiftmac}.  It remains a compatibility alternative to
Omnivox's AVSpeechSynthesizer engine; see
@code{servers/mac-swiftmac/Readme.org}.
@item mac
The legacy Python/PyObjC macOS server.  The unset runtime selector still names
it as a compatibility default on Darwin@comma{} but it is not the recommended or
documented initial-user route.  It depends on Foundation@comma{} AppKit@comma{} and
PyObjCTools availability.
@item outloud
The direct GNU/Linux IBM TTS or Eloquence adapter.  It requires a separately
licensed compatible runtime@comma{} which Emacsvox does not distribute.  Voxin
availability is documented at @uref{https://voxin.oralux.net}.
@item dtk-soft
The direct Software DECtalk adapter.  Install its runtime separately and see
@code{servers/software-dtk/Readme.org}.  Emacsvox does not distribute licensed
speech runtimes.
@end table

The native Windows audio@comma{} standalone Eloquence@comma{} and standalone DECtalk bridges
under @code{servers/} are advanced WSL-specific compatibility paths.  Piper is
an optional Omnivox engine and model workflow@comma{} not a primary Emacsvox server.

@node Backend Diagnostics And Ownership
@section Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities

Use the boundary at which speech failed to choose the next diagnostic:

@table @asis
@item @code{omnivox} is not found
Under WSL2@comma{} run @code{./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check} and confirm its persistent
path.  On other POSIX systems@comma{} check @code{PATH}; an absolute @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} can
identify the installed executable.  Keep its adjacent @code{espeak-ng-data} and
@code{third-party-licenses} directories with it.
@item @code{omnivox --check} reports an error or is inaudible
The failure is below Emacsvox.  Consult Omnivox's
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/README.md, build guide}@comma{}
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/STATUS.md, platform status}@comma{}
and
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/DIAGNOSTICS.md, diagnostics guide}.  Do not diagnose Emacs configuration until the tone and
speech check passes.
@item The check passes but Emacsvox is silent
Confirm that @code{TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox} was present before Emacsvox loaded@comma{} then
use @code{M-x tts-speak-version}.  Under WSL2 with a release binary@comma{} rerun
@code{./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check}.  For a source-built development
runtime@comma{} run @code{make verify-windows-omnivox-runtime} and restart the speech
process after restaging.
@item A process starts and later fails
The Emacsvox POSIX/WSL launcher writes private@comma{} bounded stderr logs below
@code{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}@comma{} defaulting to
@code{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}.  Spoken text is excluded unless
@code{OMNIVOX_LOG_SYNTHESIS_TEXT} is explicitly enabled; enabling it can expose
passwords@comma{} messages@comma{} and document contents.
@item Speech works but feels delayed
Press @code{C-e d D} to enable a private diagnostic log@comma{} then reproduce one short
presentation and press @code{C-e d D} again to disable logging.  The default file
is @code{~/.emacsvox-aural-diagnostics.log}; use @code{C-u C-e d D} when enabling to
choose another file.  Its @code{dispatch-sent}@comma{} @code{mixer-source-observed}@comma{} and
@code{playback-terminal-observed} records share an Omnivox dispatch ID@.  Match
that ID in the ordinary Omnivox session log to separate Emacs submission@comma{}
server queueing@comma{} synthesis@comma{} and mixer-source delay.  The Emacs log is
disabled by default because its submission records include complete spoken
text.  A mixer-source observation is not physical audible output; device and
hardware buffers can add latency afterward.
@end table

Server selection@comma{} the POSIX/WSL launcher@comma{} Windows staging@comma{} and the bundled
Lisp adapter are implemented and documented in Emacsvox.  Native builds@comma{}
engines@comma{} the command line@comma{} runtime data layout@comma{} and protocol specifications
are documented in the Omnivox repository.  Backend-local READMEs describe
licensed runtimes and compatibility adapters.  Follow those sources for
versioned details rather than copying their contracts into installation
instructions.

@node Audio Desktop
@chapter Work With The Audio Desktop

@cindex Audio Desktop

The @emph{audio desktop} is the Emacsvox working environment.  It is not a
separate program or a spoken copy of a graphical desktop.  Emacs keeps each
document@comma{} application@comma{} and result in a buffer; its modes understand the kind
of information in that buffer; and Emacsvox presents that context through
speech@comma{} voice changes@comma{} sounds@comma{} and structured navigation.

You can begin without learning a new desktop model.  Open a file or application
normally@comma{} use @kbd{C-e m} to hear the current buffer and mode@comma{} and press
@kbd{C-h m} to discover what that buffer can do.  The rest of this chapter
explains why the same approach works for text@comma{} Web pages@comma{} mail@comma{} shells@comma{} source
code@comma{} and many other tasks.

@menu
* Desktop Objects::              Understand and move between Emacs buffers.
* Object Oriented Desktop::      Let major and minor modes interpret each kind of content.
* Context-Sensitive Interaction:: Use aural structure, mode commands, buffers, and search.
@end menu

@node Desktop Objects
@section Buffers Hold Your Work

@cindex buffers

A buffer is Emacs's working view of one piece of information.  A buffer might
contain a file you are editing@comma{} an Info manual@comma{} a directory listing@comma{} a Web
page@comma{} a mail search@comma{} a shell session@comma{} or a temporary Help result.  Some buffers
are connected to files; many useful application buffers are not.

The mode line identifies the selected buffer and its major mode.  Press
@kbd{C-e m} whenever you want to hear it.  Common buffer commands include:

@table @kbd
@item C-x b
Switch to another buffer by name.  Completion lets you narrow the choices
instead of remembering the complete name.

@item C-x C-b
Open a buffer list.  In that list@comma{} use @kbd{C-h m} to learn its navigation@comma{}
selection@comma{} saving@comma{} and cleanup commands.

@item C-x k
Close a buffer that you no longer need.  Emacs asks before discarding
unsaved file changes.
@end table

Moving to another buffer changes the selected work object without closing the
one you left.  This makes it practical to keep a document@comma{} its reference
material@comma{} a shell@comma{} and related application results available together.

@node Object Oriented Desktop
@section Let Modes Give Each Buffer Its Tools

@cindex major modes

@cindex minor modes

Every buffer has one major mode that provides its main editing or application
behavior.  A source buffer uses a programming-language mode@comma{} Dired uses a mode
for directories@comma{} EWW uses one for Web pages@comma{} and Org uses one for structured
notes and documents.  Minor modes add optional behavior alongside the major
mode.

This context is what makes Emacs efficient: a movement command can move by a
sentence in prose@comma{} a heading in an outline@comma{} an item in a list@comma{} or another
meaningful unit supplied by the active mode.  Emacsvox speaks the result of the
operation and can use the same context to announce state changes.

You do not need to predict which commands every mode provides.  Press
@kbd{C-h m} in the buffer you are using@comma{} then @kbd{C-h k} followed by a key
when you want a fuller explanation.  @xref{Using Online Help} provides a
short practical guide to these discovery commands.

@node Context-Sensitive Interaction
@section Hear And Navigate The Current Context

@cindex Context-Sensitive Interaction

Emacsvox uses information already exposed by Emacs and the active application.
That produces more useful feedback than reading every visible character in the
same way.  You can hear what an object means@comma{} move using its structure@comma{} and ask
for detail only when you need it.

@subsection Hear Structure Without Extra Words

@cindex audio formatting

Voice changes can distinguish headings@comma{} comments@comma{} strings@comma{} links@comma{} warnings@comma{}
and other meaningful text.  Short sounds and tones can mark events or state
changes without interrupting the spoken content.  Ordinary text remains
available through the usual reading commands.

The exact result depends on the mode@comma{} the Emacsvox integration@comma{} and your Aural
Presentation settings.  @xref{Aural Presentation} explains how the
parts fit together@comma{} including the established Voice Lock compatibility layer;
@kbd{C-e E} explains the presentation chosen for the object at point.

@subsection Move By The Structure Of The Content

@cindex structured navigation

Prefer the navigation supplied by the active mode when it matches your task.
For example@comma{} Info moves among nodes and menu entries@comma{} Org and Outline move
among headings@comma{} Dired moves among files@comma{} programming modes understand
definitions and diagnostics@comma{} and EWW exposes links and document structure.

Begin with @kbd{C-h m}.  It describes the commands available in the
running buffer@comma{} including personal customizations and installed packages.
@xref{Browsing Structure} points to maintained guides for several common
types of content.

@subsection Move Between Tasks Without Losing Your Place

@cindex desktop navigation

Buffers keep different tasks available in one Emacs session.  Use
@kbd{C-x b} to return to a known buffer or @kbd{C-x C-b} to browse the
current set.  Emacs windows can show more than one buffer at a time; if the
selected window is unclear@comma{} @kbd{C-e C-w} describes the arrangement.

Applications often create several related buffers@comma{} such as a search result and
an individual message.  Their mode help explains the commands for moving among
those views.  Treat each buffer as a place you can revisit@comma{} rather than as a
dialog that must be completed before doing anything else.

@subsection Search Wherever You Are

@cindex searching

@cindex finding

In most text buffers@comma{} @kbd{C-s} searches forward incrementally and
@kbd{C-r} searches backward.  Type more text to narrow the match@comma{} repeat the
search key to move to another match@comma{} and press @kbd{C-g} to cancel.  This same
basic search is available in documents@comma{} Help@comma{} source code@comma{} and many application
results.

Also look for the mode's semantic search and filtering commands.  A mail
application can search message fields@comma{} Dired can narrow files@comma{} and a browser
can move directly among links or headings.  Use the general search when text
is what matters and the mode-specific command when the kind of object matters.

@node Aural Presentation
@chapter Aural Presentation: Speech@comma{} Voices@comma{} And Sound

@cindex Aural Presentation

Aural Presentation is the Emacsvox system that decides @emph{how} information and
events should be heard.  It can combine spoken text@comma{} voice changes@comma{} auditory
icons@comma{} tones@comma{} pauses@comma{} and spatial cues in one ordered presentation.  The speech
server delivers that result; it does not decide what a heading@comma{} unread message@comma{}
completed command@comma{} warning@comma{} or other object means.

You do not need to configure Aural Presentation before using Emacsvox.  The
built-in baseline and application integrations provide useful defaults.  When
you do want a change@comma{} start from the object you heard and use Aural Home rather
than editing configuration data by hand:

@table @kbd
@item C-e H
Open Aural Home@comma{} the spoken control center for understanding@comma{} adjusting@comma{} and
diagnosing presentation.

@item C-e E
Explain why the object at point received its voices@comma{} sounds@comma{} and other
actions.
@end table

This chapter gives a user-level model of the system and practical workflows for
common changes.  The generated reference remains the authority for rule
schemas@comma{} transport contracts@comma{} and extension development.

@menu
* Presentation Model::           Follow meaning from an Emacs application to audible output.
* Presentation Modalities::      Recognize speech, voices, cues, tones, pauses, and spatial output.
* Aural Home::                   Navigate the spoken presentation control center.
* Explaining Presentation::      Explain current or recently queued feedback.
* Adjusting Presentation::       Preview, scope, save, and undo presentation changes.
* Presentation Profiles::        Save and switch complete presentation configurations.
* Voices And Routing::           Distinguish logical styles, palettes, physical voices, and engines.
* Sounds And Space::             Audition sound packs and inspect spatial fallback.
* Presentation Troubleshooting:: Trace unexpected output and use safe diagnostics.
* Voice Lock Compatibility: Voice Lock.  Understand the established face and personality voice adapter.
@end menu

@node Presentation Model
@section A Practical Model Of Presentation

@cindex presentation model

It helps to separate four questions that are often mixed together:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.22 0.30 0.48
@item Layer
@tab Question
@tab Example
@item Meaning
@tab What happened or what is this?
@tab A level-two heading was reached during navigation.
@item Presentation
@tab What should convey that meaning?
@tab Speak the heading in a named voice and play a section cue first.
@item Resources
@tab Which voice and sound realize that request?
@tab Resolve the logical voice through the active palette and the cue through the active sound pack.
@item Output
@tab What can this machine produce?
@tab Route speech to an installed engine and apply supported spatial behavior.
@end multitable

An Emacs mode or Emacsvox integration supplies facts about an object or event.
Facts can describe a role such as a heading@comma{} an event such as completion@comma{} a
state such as unread@comma{} or an attribute such as heading level.  Context also says
whether you are navigating@comma{} reading continuously@comma{} or receiving a state change.
This allows the same object to receive concise feedback during continuous
reading and more orientation when you move to it deliberately.

Presentation rules match those facts and context.  A rule can choose a voice@comma{}
add speech or a cue@comma{} insert a pause or tone@comma{} request spatial placement@comma{} or
suppress something that is unhelpful.  Several matching rules can compose; a
stronger@comma{} narrower rule need change only the relevant part rather than repeat
the complete presentation.

The user-visible configuration layers are:

@enumerate
@item
A fixed internal baseline that preserves established Emacsvox behavior.

@item
Automatic integration behavior supplied by the active mode or package.

@item
Enabled @emph{Presentation Options} that add optional behavior in a stable
weakest-to-strongest order.

@item
Personal@comma{} session@comma{} and current-buffer @emph{overrides}.  These are the strongest
layers and are intended for precise changes.
@end enumerate

A presentation profile is not another rule layer.  It records a complete set
of selected options@comma{} sound pack@comma{} voice palette@comma{} and spatial choices so you can
switch those choices together.

@node Presentation Modalities
@section What You Can Hear

@cindex presentation modalities

Aural Presentation can use one or more of these forms of feedback:

@itemize
@item
@emph{Speech} conveys content@comma{} labels@comma{} prompts@comma{} results@comma{} and status.  Added speech
should explain meaning that is not already clear from the content.

@item
@emph{Voice} changes characteristics of spoken content.  Named logical voices
make distinctions such as headings@comma{} annotations@comma{} warnings@comma{} or quoted text
portable across speech engines.

@item
@emph{Auditory cues} are short recognizable sounds associated with semantic
events or objects.  A sound pack supplies the concrete audio for those cue
names.

@item
@emph{Tones} convey compact information using a named pitch and duration.  They
are useful when a short distinction is preferable to speech or a sampled
sound.

@item
@emph{Pauses} separate related parts of a presentation without adding words.

@item
@emph{Spatial presentation} can place speech or cues across supported output.
When the selected backend cannot provide the requested placement@comma{} Emacsvox
keeps the content audible using its documented fallback.
@end itemize

These forms can be ordered around the content.  For example@comma{} entering a section
might play a cue@comma{} speak the heading in a distinctive voice@comma{} and leave a short
pause before body text.  The presentation is one result@comma{} not several unrelated
notifications.

The precise sound may vary by speech engine@comma{} installed voices@comma{} sound pack@comma{} and
audio hardware.  The intended distinction should remain understandable even
when a simpler backend uses fewer voice characteristics or centered output.

@node Aural Home
@section Begin With Aural Home

@cindex Aural Home

Open Aural Home from an ordinary buffer with @kbd{C-e H}.  Emacsvox
remembers that source buffer so contextual actions such as explanation and
remapping can operate on the object you were using.

Aural Home is a spoken table.  Each row names an area@comma{} reports its current
status@comma{} and describes its purpose.  The common controls are:

@table @kbd
@item n@comma{} p@comma{} down@comma{} up
Move by row and speak the new row.
@item left@comma{} right
Move among the titled columns.
@item .
Speak the current column title and value.
@item SPC
Speak the complete current row.
@item RET
Open or perform the current row's main action.
@item ?
Display and speak help for the current Aural interface.
@item h
Return to Aural Home from a manager or editor.
@item q
Leave the current Aural interface.
@end table

The rows fall into a few practical groups:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.27 0.73
@item Goal
@tab Aural Home routes
@item Understand feedback
@tab Explain At Point@comma{} Recent Aural Feedback@comma{} Semantic Vocabulary@comma{} and Training Mode.
@item Change one behavior
@tab Remap Voice At Point@comma{} Remap Earcon At Point@comma{} Presentation Overrides@comma{} and Current Buffer Rules.
@item Select broader behavior
@tab Presentation Options and Presentation Profiles.
@item Adjust resources
@tab Voice Palettes@comma{} Voice Workbench@comma{} Sound Packs@comma{} Spatial Capabilities@comma{} and Spatial Settings.
@item Diagnose the system
@tab Aural Doctor.
@end multitable

You can press @key{RET} on a row without memorizing its shortcut.  Aural
Home also provides direct keys for frequent routes@comma{} including @kbd{x} for
explanation@comma{} @kbd{O} for overrides@comma{} @kbd{H} for recent feedback@comma{}
@kbd{P} for profiles@comma{} @kbd{V} for voice palettes@comma{} @kbd{W} for
Voice Workbench@comma{} and @kbd{D} for Aural Doctor.

@node Explaining Presentation
@section Understand Why Something Sounded That Way

@cindex explaining presentation

Use this workflow when feedback is surprising@comma{} unclear@comma{} or especially useful:

@enumerate
@item
Leave point on the object and press @kbd{C-e E}.

@item
Read the explanation as a report of the object and occasion@comma{} matching
presentation rules@comma{} selected actions@comma{} concrete voices and sounds@comma{} and any
backend fallback or degradation.

@item
Note the strongest rule that changed the part you care about.  This tells
you whether to inspect an option@comma{} an override@comma{} a voice route@comma{} a sound pack@comma{}
or the Voice Lock compatibility layer.
@end enumerate

When a retained presentation from that source is available@comma{} the explanation
describes the exact output that was queued@comma{} including its source location and
resolved resources.  It does not reinterpret that old output using your latest
settings.  Without an exact record@comma{} Emacsvox labels the explanation as a
simulation of what the current rules would produce at point.

To inspect something heard earlier@comma{} open Aural Home and choose @emph{Recent Aural
Feedback}@comma{} or press @kbd{H} there.  Each row is a bounded frozen record.
Useful commands in the feedback browser include:

@table @kbd
@item RET or e
Explain the selected retained presentation.
@item P
Replay its complete ordered presentation.
@item c
Audition only its auditory cues.
@item r
Prepare a voice remap from the selected record.
@item R
Replace@comma{} suppress@comma{} or restore one exact earcon.
@item L
Change the bounded history limit for the current session.
@end table

Training Mode@comma{} available from Aural Home@comma{} speaks concise semantic explanations
after normal presentations.  It can help while learning the vocabulary used by
an integration@comma{} but is intentionally more verbose than ordinary operation;
turn it off when you no longer need the additional labels.

@node Adjusting Presentation
@section Make A Small Change Safely

@cindex presentation overrides

Start by choosing the narrowest tool that matches your intention:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.42 0.30 0.28
@item Intention
@tab Use
@tab Scope
@item Try an optional behavior supplied by Emacsvox or an integration
@tab Presentation Options
@tab Global configuration
@item Change the voice or earcon for the object at point
@tab Remap At Point
@tab Personal@comma{} session@comma{} or current buffer
@item Review or remove an existing precise change
@tab Presentation Overrides
@tab Its recorded scope
@item Try rules only in the current work buffer
@tab Current Buffer Rules
@tab Life of that buffer
@item Switch a complete reading or working setup
@tab Presentation Profiles
@tab Saved global configuration
@end multitable

@subsection Remap One Item At Point

@enumerate
@item
Move to a representative object and press @kbd{C-e E} so you know what
currently controls it.

@item
Press @kbd{C-e H}.  Use @kbd{r} to remap its voice or @kbd{R} to
remap one exact earcon.

@item
Choose the scope deliberately:

@itemize
@item
a @emph{personal} override persists across Emacs sessions;
@item
a @emph{session} override lasts until this Emacs exits; and
@item
a @emph{buffer} override affects only the remembered live buffer.
@end itemize

@item
Review the generated rule in the editor.  It is initially unwritten.  Use
the editor's @kbd{?} help@comma{} preview when offered@comma{} and press @kbd{w} only
when the target@comma{} scope@comma{} and change are correct.

@item
Return to the source@comma{} repeat the action@comma{} and use @kbd{C-e E} again to
confirm which rule now wins.
@end enumerate

Open @emph{Presentation Overrides} to manage these changes together.  There@comma{}
@kbd{P} previews the complete current result@comma{} @kbd{t} temporarily disables
or enables a rule@comma{} and @kbd{d} removes it.  Disabling or deleting an
override restores the next weaker behavior; it does not leave a copied default
behind.

@subsection Try A Presentation Option

Presentation Options are maintained additions such as richer heading feedback
or integration-specific cues.  From Aural Home@comma{} open @emph{Presentation Options}:

@enumerate
@item
Use @key{TAB} or @key{RET} on a collection to expand it.

@item
Move to an option and press @kbd{P} to preview it composed with the
current configuration.  Press @kbd{C-u P} to preview it in isolation.

@item
Press @kbd{t} to enable or disable it.  Press @kbd{a} to hear
enabled options in their exact weakest-to-strongest order.

@item
Return to the source and test the original task.  Personal@comma{} session@comma{} and
buffer overrides remain stronger than an enabled option.
@end enumerate

Avoid reordering options merely to solve one exceptional object.  A narrow
override usually expresses that intention more clearly.

@node Presentation Profiles
@section Save Complete Setups With Profiles

@cindex presentation profiles

A presentation profile records a coordinated setup: enabled Presentation
Options and their order@comma{} the sound pack@comma{} the voice palette@comma{} and portable
spatial settings.  This is useful for switching between focused work and
leisure reading@comma{} between quiet and information-rich feedback@comma{} or between audio
environments.

To save and use a profile:

@enumerate
@item
Configure the live options@comma{} sound pack@comma{} voice palette@comma{} and spatial choices
you want.

@item
Open @emph{Presentation Profiles} from Aural Home or press @kbd{P} there.

@item
Press @kbd{N} to save the current live configuration as a new named
profile.

@item
Later@comma{} select that profile and press @key{RET} or @kbd{a} to apply
its saved values.

@item
If you deliberately change the live configuration and want the selected
profile to adopt it@comma{} press @kbd{w}.  If you instead want to discard the
live differences@comma{} press @kbd{a} to restore the saved profile; Emacsvox
asks before replacing the diverged values.
@end enumerate

Only one profile identity is selected.  @emph{Active} means the selected profile's
saved values match the live configuration.  @emph{Diverged} means the same profile
is still selected but the live values differ; it is a status@comma{} not an unsaved
editor warning.

Profiles do not copy presentation rules.  Edit rules in Presentation Options
or Overrides.  They also do not capture buffer-local Voice Lock state or
current-buffer rules@comma{} because those belong to an individual buffer rather than
a global setup.

@node Voices And Routing
@section Choose Voices And Speech Engines

@cindex voice palettes

@cindex Voice Workbench

Four terms help when adjusting voices:

@itemize
@item
A @emph{logical voice} expresses a purpose such as boldening@comma{} annotation@comma{} or a
heading distinction without naming one synthesizer voice.

@item
A @emph{voice palette} gives named logical voices portable style characteristics.
Palettes can inherit from another palette and travel between machines.

@item
A @emph{physical voice} is an actual voice reported by an installed speech engine.
Its exact identifier is usually machine-local.

@item
An @emph{engine route} decides which engine and physical voice realize a logical
voice on the current machine.
@end itemize

Use the @emph{Voice Palettes} manager when you want to choose or edit the portable
style vocabulary.  Press @key{RET} on a palette to browse its voices@comma{}
preview them before editing@comma{} and use @kbd{a} in the palette manager to
activate a selected palette.  Built-in palettes remain intact; editing can
create a personal copy.

Use @emph{Voice Workbench} when the question involves installed voices@comma{} engines@comma{} or
routes.  Open it from Aural Home with @kbd{W}.  Its main views are:

@table @kbd
@item l
Logical voices and their current routes.
@item v
Physical voices reported by the engines.
@item e
Engines@comma{} preference@comma{} fallback@comma{} and health.
@item s
Portable styles and effects.
@end table

Press @kbd{P} to preview a row@comma{} @kbd{A} to preview all visible
voices@comma{} @kbd{B} to compare two@comma{} and @kbd{S} to stop preview.
Use @kbd{a} to assign a physical voice and @kbd{t} to tune the
selected logical voice on its effective route.  Route changes are staged until
you press @kbd{w} or @kbd{C-c C-c} to save and apply them.  Pressing
@kbd{q} with unsaved route changes warns that they remain staged.

For a quick engine preference@comma{} @kbd{C-e d e} chooses an available engine
for the current Emacs session.  Press @kbd{C-u} first to save the new
preference.  Explicit logical-voice routes still take precedence.

Judge voices in context.  The goal is usually to distinguish nearby kinds of
content@comma{} not to identify every named voice in isolation.  Use a portable
palette for the intended distinction and machine routing for the best local
realization.

@node Sounds And Space
@section Choose Sounds And Spatial Cues

@cindex sound packs

@cindex spatial presentation

Presentation rules refer to auditory cues by semantic name.  A sound pack maps
those names to concrete audio assets and can inherit missing cues from another
pack.  This separation lets you change the overall sound character without
rewriting every presentation rule.

Open @emph{Sound Packs} from Aural Home.  In the manager:

@table @kbd
@item P
Audition a representative cue without activating the pack.
@item RET
Browse the pack's individual cues and their source or fallback.
@item P or RET in the cue browser
Audition the selected concrete cue.
@item v
Validate the pack and report missing or invalid resources.
@item a
Activate the selected pack.
@end table

Use a sound pack when you want to change a family of cues together.  Use
@emph{Remap Earcon At Point} or Presentation Overrides when one semantic event needs
a different cue@comma{} suppression@comma{} or restoration.

Spatial requests are portable descriptions rather than assumptions about one
device.  Aural Home separates @emph{Spatial Capabilities}@comma{} which reports what the
current route can provide and how it falls back@comma{} from @emph{Spatial Settings}@comma{} which
controls output@comma{} separation@comma{} direction@comma{} speech@comma{} and cues.  Unsupported or mono
output remains audible at the center rather than silently losing content.

Named tones are another compact presentation resource.  They do not require a
sampled sound file and may be used independently of the active sound pack.

@node Presentation Troubleshooting
@section Diagnose Unexpected Presentation

@cindex Aural Doctor

Use the symptom to choose the shortest diagnostic route:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.43 0.57
@item Symptom
@tab First action
@item The current object sounds wrong or too verbose
@tab Press @kbd{C-e E} and inspect the strongest matching rule.
@item Something heard earlier was wrong
@tab Open Recent Aural Feedback and explain its frozen record.
@item A broad optional behavior is unwanted
@tab Inspect Presentation Options and preview or disable the matching option.
@item One narrow exception is unwanted
@tab Open Presentation Overrides@comma{} preview it@comma{} then disable or remove it.
@item A logical voice uses the wrong engine or physical voice
@tab Inspect its route and engine health in Voice Workbench.
@item A cue is missing or inappropriate
@tab Validate and audition the active Sound Pack@comma{} then inspect earcon overrides.
@item A saved setup no longer appears active
@tab Check whether the selected Presentation Profile is active or diverged.
@item Voice changes disappear only in some buffers
@tab Compare Voice Lock and explicit face-presentation state.
@end multitable

Open @emph{Aural Doctor} from Aural Home or press @kbd{D} there.  The doctor
checks live bindings@comma{} loaded source or byte-code@comma{} the compatibility baseline@comma{}
options@comma{} profiles@comma{} personal data@comma{} sound resources@comma{} spatial fallback@comma{} and the
speech backend.  It does not start the speech server or change settings
automatically.  When one row offers a reviewed safe repair@comma{} press @kbd{r}
on that row to run it.

Press @kbd{?} in any Aural interface for its complete current keys.  If
speech itself is absent rather than merely presented incorrectly@comma{} follow
@ref{Backend Diagnostics And Ownership,Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities}; presentation diagnostics assume that a speech route can run.

@node Voice Lock
@section Voice Lock Compatibility

@cindex Voice Lock

Voice Lock is one compatibility input to Aural Presentation@comma{} not the whole
presentation system.  It maps established Emacs faces and @code{personality} text
properties to voices.  Emacs calls its visual style labels @emph{faces}; Emacsvox
can use those labels without requiring you to see them.

The three related policies are independent:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.28 0.42 0.30
@item Policy
@tab What it controls
@tab How to inspect or change it
@item Semantic presentation
@tab Rules based on roles@comma{} events@comma{} states@comma{} and attributes supplied by integrations.
@tab Use @kbd{C-e E}@comma{} Presentation Options@comma{} and Overrides.
@item Explicit face presentation
@tab Aural rules that deliberately match a named Emacs face.
@tab Run @code{emacsvox-aural-toggle-face-presentation} or inspect it in Aural Doctor.
@item Voice Lock
@tab The historical face or personality-to-voice mapping in each buffer.
@tab Use @kbd{C-e d v} locally or @kbd{C-e d C-v} globally.
@end multitable

To find out whether a distinction comes from Voice Lock:

@enumerate
@item
Move across two elements that the mode treats differently@comma{} such as a comment
and string in source code or a heading and link in Org.

@item
Press @kbd{C-e d v} to toggle Voice Lock only in that buffer@comma{} then
compare the same elements.

@item
Press @kbd{C-e E} on the element.  The explanation reports the
Voice Lock and explicit face-presentation state alongside the rules that
contributed to the result.
@end enumerate

Turning Voice Lock off removes only its compatibility voice contribution.  It
does not disable semantic presentation@comma{} explicit face rules@comma{} auditory cues@comma{}
tones@comma{} or ordinary spoken status.  The global Voice Lock command updates
existing and new buffers; an individual mode can still opt out when it starts.
Buffer-local Voice Lock state is not saved in a presentation profile.

For new customization@comma{} prefer explanation@comma{} Aural Home@comma{} voice palettes@comma{} and
Voice Workbench.  Older configurations can still customize a named legacy
voice's @code{-settings} option@comma{} such as @code{voice-monotone-settings}.  Customize the
settings option rather than a generated @code{acss-...} voice symbol.  Historical
background is preserved in
@ref{Audio Formatting And Aural CSS,,,emacsvox-heritage,Emacspeak Heritage}.

@section Further Detail

Every Aural manager and editor has live @kbd{?} help.  The generated
@ref{Emacsvox Commands And Options,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} records the exact public commands and options.  The source-tree file
@code{etc/aural-presentation-reference.org} documents the rule schema@comma{} selection
order@comma{} queue behavior@comma{} extension contracts@comma{} and advanced data authoring.  Those
details are useful when developing an integration or a reusable Presentation
Option; they are not required for ordinary customization.

@node Using Online Help
@chapter Find Help While You Work

@cindex Online Help

You do not need to memorize every Emacs or Emacsvox command.  Emacs can explain
the key you are about to use@comma{} the modes active in the current buffer@comma{} and the
commands and variables available in your running session.  This live help is
usually more reliable than a copied key list because it includes installed
packages and personal customizations.

Most help commands begin with the @kbd{C-h} prefix.  If you forget what can
follow it@comma{} press @kbd{C-h C-h} for @code{help-for-help}.

@section Ask About A Key@comma{} Command@comma{} Or Setting

Use the question you have to choose a help command:

@itemize
@item
@kbd{C-h k} followed by a key runs @code{describe-key} and explains the
command currently bound to that key.

@item
@kbd{C-h f} runs @code{describe-function}.  Enter a command or function name
to read its complete documentation.

@item
@kbd{C-h v} runs @code{describe-variable}.  Enter an option name to hear its
current value and documentation.

@item
@kbd{C-h w} runs @code{where-is} and reports the keys that invoke a command.
@end itemize

For example@comma{} press @kbd{C-h k C-e l} to ask what the current-line speaking
key does.  If you know the command name but not its key@comma{} use @kbd{C-h w} and
enter that name.

@section Discover What The Current Buffer Can Do

Two commands answer most questions about a buffer:

@itemize
@item
@kbd{C-h m} runs @code{describe-mode}.  It explains the major mode@comma{} active
minor modes@comma{} and their local commands.

@item
@kbd{C-h b} runs @code{describe-bindings}.  It lists the effective key
bindings after local package maps and personal customizations are applied.
@end itemize

After opening Dired@comma{} EWW@comma{} mail@comma{} a terminal@comma{} or another application@comma{} start with
@kbd{C-h m}.  Use @kbd{C-h b} when you need the exact keys available in
that particular buffer.

@section Read And Leave A Help Window

Help usually appears in a separate @code{*Help*} window@comma{} and Emacsvox speaks the
most relevant initial result automatically.  Press @kbd{C-e h}
@code{emacsvox-speak-help} to hear the displayed help again.

To explore the help text directly@comma{} press @kbd{C-x o} to move to its window
and use ordinary reading and navigation commands.  Press @kbd{q} in the Help
buffer when you are finished.  If another window is selected unexpectedly@comma{}
@kbd{C-e C-w} describes the current window arrangement.

@section Learn Interactively And Read Manuals

@itemize
@item
@kbd{C-h t} opens the interactive Emacs tutorial.  Emacsvox speaks the
tutorial and your navigation as you work through it.

@item
@kbd{C-h i} opens Info@comma{} the manual reader used by Emacs and Emacsvox.
Press @kbd{d} for the Info directory@comma{} then @kbd{m} and enter @code{Emacsvox}
to open this manual.

@item
@kbd{C-h C-l} runs @code{emacsvox-learn-emacs}@comma{} a learning loop that speaks
the function of each key you press.  Leave the loop with @kbd{C-g}.
@end itemize

See the Emacs manual's help and Info chapters when you want to search manuals@comma{}
follow cross-references@comma{} or keep a help buffer for later.

@section Emacsvox-Specific Help

@itemize
@item
@kbd{C-h C-e} @code{emacsvox-describe-emacsvox} opens a concise Emacsvox
overview.

@item
@kbd{C-e N} @code{emacsvox-view-emacsvox-news} opens versioned project news@comma{}
while @kbd{C-e T} @code{emacsvox-view-emacsvox-tips} opens productivity tips.

@item
@kbd{C-e H} @code{emacsvox-aural} opens Aural Home for presentation options.

@item
@kbd{C-e E} @code{emacsvox-aural-explain-presentation} explains why the
object at point received its voices@comma{} sounds@comma{} and other auditory actions.
@end itemize

The generated
@ref{Emacsvox Keymaps,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} records all default Emacsvox bindings.  Prefer @kbd{C-h k}@comma{}
@kbd{C-h m}@comma{} and @kbd{C-h b} when a running package or customization
changes those defaults.

@node Emacs Packages
@chapter Applications And Integrations

@cindex applications

@cindex integrations

@cindex packages, speech support

Emacsvox prepares speech support for many Emacs modes as their libraries are
loaded.  Start the application normally; when its buffer appears@comma{} use
@kbd{C-h m} for the active modes and their commands@comma{} @kbd{C-h b} for the
effective bindings@comma{} and @kbd{C-h k} followed by a key to identify one binding.
The generated
@ref{Emacsvox Commands And Options,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} records the public Emacsvox commands and options.

This chapter is a curated starting point@comma{} not a promise that every module in
the generated reference has a maintained task guide.  Emacs itself or a
third-party package provides the application's general operation; Emacsvox
adds the speech feedback.  Consult both sets of help when diagnosing a problem.

Earlier Emacspeak surveys@comma{} including their W3@comma{} Dismal@comma{} and scanner/OCR setup@comma{}
are preserved as historical material in
@ref{Emacs Packages,Historical Application Survey,,emacsvox-heritage,Emacspeak Heritage}.  They are not current setup instructions.

