@node Documentation Maintenance
@section Documentation Maintenance

This chapter is for contributors changing the maintained manuals@comma{} generated
reference@comma{} or public browser documentation.  Reports and internal notes do not
require an Emacsvox build; use the smallest documentation target that covers
the source being changed.

@subsection Choose the canonical source

The maintained Org master is @code{docs/manual/emacsvox.org}.  Current-user chapters
live under @code{docs/manual/chapters/}@comma{} while developer and maintainer chapters
live under @code{docs/developer/}.  The source layout and fast authoring loop are
summarized in @code{docs/manual/README.org}.

The current project overview lives in @code{README.org}@comma{} and the complete user
installation path lives in @code{docs/manual/chapters/installation.org}.  Source
checkout@comma{} byte-code@comma{} and release procedures live in
@code{docs/developer/integration-maintenance.org}.  Submit changes through the
Emacsvox repository at @uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox}.  Before
changing a governed workflow or architecture@comma{} read @code{AGENTS.md} and every
accepted record under @code{docs/adr/}.  Repository-local @code{local.mk} selects the
Emacs used by supported targets; Emacsvox requires Emacs 31 or newer.

Do not hand-edit generated references.  @code{info/docs.texi} and @code{info/keys.texi}
come from the source documentation generator@comma{} and
@code{etc/aural-presentation-reference.org} comes from
@code{utils/emacsvox-aural-audit.el}.  The first two sources form the separately
installed @code{emacsvox-reference.info} manual.  Selected historical chapters form
the separately installed @code{emacsvox-heritage.info} manual.

@subsection Use the fast authoring workflow

Keep current operating instructions separate from historical accounts.  State
which backend or platform a procedure applies to@comma{} avoid duplicating detailed
protocol contracts across repositories@comma{} and link to the owning document.
Apply the user-versus-developer audience test in ADR 0004 before choosing a
source directory.

While editing Org prose@comma{} preview the complete manual without inspecting or
rebuilding Lisp byte-code:

@example
make docs-org-preview
@end example

The ignored Info@comma{} Texinfo@comma{} and HTML previews appear under
@code{.docs-preview/org-manual/}.  Once the text settles@comma{} run @code{make docs-update} to
update the tracked generated Texinfo body and checked Info files.  Do not edit
those generated files directly.

When public Lisp documentation or keys change@comma{} first bring byte-code current
with @code{make bytecode}.  Use @code{make bytecode-rebuild} instead after changing
macros or public interfaces@comma{} as described in @code{AGENTS.md}.  Then run
@code{make docs-reference}.  @code{make docs-generate} remains the comprehensive
reference and manual update.

Before review@comma{} merge@comma{} or deployment@comma{} run the non-mutating
@code{make docs-release-check}; @code{make docs-check} is its compatibility alias.
Commit the regenerated Info artifacts with their source changes.

@subsection Publish the browser manual

Org on @code{master} is the canonical maintained manual source.  GitHub Pages serves
the generated static files committed on the separate @code{gh-pages} branch; never
edit those HTML files as source.  Publishing is deliberately explicit:

@enumerate
@item
Finish the manual change@comma{} update its checked Info output@comma{} run the release
validation target above@comma{} and commit the complete source-side change on
@code{master}.

@item
Check out that exact commit in a clean source worktree.  Build its local@comma{}
ignored byte-code with the Emacs selected by @code{local.mk} or an @code{EMACS=}
override.  Use a separate sibling worktree checked out on @code{gh-pages} as the
publication directory.

@item
From the clean source worktree@comma{} run:

@example
make docs-publish-pages DOCS_PUBLISH_DIR=/path/to/emacsvox-pages
@end example

The target validates the manual and publishes that same staged render@comma{}
replaces only its managed HTML files@comma{} writes @code{.nojekyll}@comma{} and records the
source commit@comma{} Emacs version@comma{} and Texinfo version in
@code{emacsvox-source.txt}.  It refuses an uncommitted source tree.

@item
Inspect the @code{gh-pages} worktree@comma{} including the provenance file and the main
browser entry points.  Commit the generated site with the source commit in
the commit message@comma{} then push @code{master} and @code{gh-pages}.  The GitHub Pages
source setting must name the root of @code{gh-pages}.
@end enumerate

No documentation target creates a commit or pushes a branch.  This keeps the
published bytes reviewable and makes publication a distinct maintainer action.

@node Speech Server Protocols
@section Speech Server Protocols

Emacsvox produces spoken output by communicating with one of several speech
servers.  Two protocol families coexist:

@itemize
@item
The legacy command-and-queue line protocol is implemented by standalone Tcl
servers and retained by Omnivox for compatibility.

@item
Capability-negotiated Omnivox control@comma{} marker@comma{} logical-voice@comma{} and structured
presentation protocols carry bounded JSON records beside that legacy stream.
@end itemize

This chapter is primarily for speech-server and client developers.  The
detailed Omnivox contracts are owned by the Omnivox repository; this manual
summarizes their relationship to Emacsvox and then documents the legacy line
protocol.

For additional notes on how to log and view @acronym{TTS} server
commands when developing a speech server@comma{} see
the historical Emacspeak article at
@uref{http://emacspeak.blogspot.com/2015/04/howto-log-speech-server-output-to-aid.html}.

@subsection Omnivox Structured Protocol

At process startup the Emacsvox adapter requests capabilities and live engine
inventory.  Features are used only after negotiation; an older server
continues on the legacy path.  Current Emacsvox requires
@code{presentation_timeline_v3} before it submits an Aural Presentation as a
structured timeline.

Concrete Aural plans retain a bounded compatibility lowering when those
features are absent.  Speech@comma{} voice commands@comma{} silence@comma{} tones@comma{} dispatch@comma{} and
interruption use the established legacy queue commands.  A legacy tone cannot
preserve the explicit overlay clock: both insert and overlay modes become an
ordered @code{t} command@comma{} and a fractional frequency is rounded to the nearest
hertz.  The surrounding speech continues rather than being rejected for that
loss of presentation precision.

Guarantee-bearing operations do not invent success on a legacy server.
Playback completion@comma{} source markers@comma{} and domain-scoped replacement remain
unavailable when the selected server cannot acknowledge them truthfully.

A complete timeline contains its delivery policy@comma{} replacement domain@comma{}
generation@comma{} dispatch ID@comma{} speech spans@comma{} and positioned actions.  Small
timelines use one bounded frame; large timelines use a bounded multipart
envelope that is validated and admitted atomically.  Decodable rejected or
stale direct timelines receive terminal status so Emacs callbacks can retire
their dispatch records.

@code{ordered} and @code{urgent} timelines are admitted immediately.  Only
matching @code{replaceable} work uses the short server-side coalescing window.
Receipt of a newer valid replaceable generation immediately cancels queued@comma{}
synthesizing@comma{} and buffered work in the same replacement domain.  It does not
globally stop ordered@comma{} urgent@comma{} legacy@comma{} or differently keyed output.

Marker events report playback start and positioned playback boundaries;
tracked status reports terminal completion.  With opt-in Aural diagnostics@comma{}
Emacs records successful dispatch send@comma{} first received @code{utterance_started}@comma{}
and terminal observation under the same dispatch ID@.  Omnivox's normal
session log records protocol admission@comma{} worker queue wait@comma{} individual engine
attempts@comma{} audio queueing@comma{} first mixer-source consumption@comma{} and terminal
playback under that ID@.  This correlation uses existing marker and terminal
records rather than adding a wire-protocol version.  Playback markers track
mixer-source consumption and may precede physical sound by the output device's
buffer latency; a protocol write or terminal callback is therefore not proof
of acoustic onset.

The canonical protocol documents are:

@itemize
@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/CONTROL-PROTOCOL.md, Omnivox Control Protocol}

@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/PRESENTATION-TIMELINE-PROTOCOL.md, Omnivox Presentation Timeline Protocol}

@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/HELPER-PROTOCOL.md, Omnivox Helper Protocol}
@end itemize

@subsection Legacy Line Protocol

The @acronym{TTS} server reads commands from standard input.
Script @strong{speech-server} can be used to cause a @acronym{TTS}
server to communicate via a @acronym{TCP} socket. Speech server
commands are used by the client application to make specific requests
of the server; the server listens for these requests in a non-blocking
read-eval-print (REPL)  loop and executes requests as they become available. Requests can
be classified as follows:

@itemize
@item
Commands that send text to be spoken.

@item
Commands that set @strong{state} of the @acronym{TTS} server.
@end itemize

All commands are of the form

@example
commandWord @{arguments@}
@end example

The braces are optional if the command argument contains no white
space.  The speech server maintains a @strong{current state} that
determines various characteristics of spoken output such as speech
rate@comma{} punctuations mode etc. (see set of commands that manipulate
speech state for complete list).  The client application
@strong{queues} The text and non-speech audio output to be produced
before asking the server to @strong{dispatch} the set of queued
requests@comma{} i.e. start producing output.

Once the server has been asked to produce output@comma{} it removes items
from the front of the queue@comma{} sends the requisite commands to the
underlying @acronym{TTS} engine@comma{} and waits for the engine to
acknowledge that the request has been completely processed. This is a
non-blocking operation@comma{} i.e.@comma{} if the client application generates
additional requests@comma{} these are processed @strong{immediately}.

This design allows a legacy Emacsvox @acronym{TTS} server to be
@strong{highly} responsive; Client applications can queue large
amounts of text (typically queued a clause at a time to achieve the
best prosody)@comma{} ask the @acronym{TTS} server to start speaking@comma{} and
interrupt the spoken output at any time.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Testing A Direct Legacy Server}Testing A Direct Legacy Server


This protocol-level test is only for a retained direct Tcl-compatible server.
Run its launcher from the Emacsvox repository root@comma{} for example:

@example
./servers/espeak
@end example

Then enter:

@example
q @{This is a test.@}
d
s
@end example

The @code{q} command queues text@comma{} @code{d} dispatches it@comma{} and @code{s} stops speech.  Type
@kbd{C-d} to close the server.  A backend-specific README may provide a
stronger diagnostic and should take precedence over this generic protocol
test.

@item
@anchor{Commands That Queue Output}Commands That Queue Output.


This section documents commands that either produce spoken output@comma{} or
queue output to be produced on demand.  Commands that place the
request on the queue are clearly marked.

@example
version
@end example

Speaks the @strong{version} of the @acronym{TTS} engine. Produces
output immediately.

@example
tts_say text
@end example

Speaks the specified @strong{text} immediately. The text is not
pre-processed in any way@comma{} contrast this with the primary way of
speaking text which is to queue text before asking the server to
process the queue.

Note that this command needs to handle the special syntax for morpheme
boundaries @code{[*]}.  The @code{[*]} syntax is specific to the
Dectalk family of synthesizers; servers for other @acronym{TTS}
engines need to map this pattern to the engine-specific code for each
engine. As an example@comma{} see @code{servers/outloud} A morpheme boundary
results in synthesizing compound words such as @strong{left bracket}
with the right intonation; using a space would result in that phrase
being synthesized as two separate words.

@example
l c
@end example

Speak @strong{c} a single character@comma{} as a letter.  The character is
spoken immediately. This command uses the @acronym{TTS} engine's
capability to speak a single character with the ability to flush
speech @strong{immediately}.  Client applications wishing to produce
character-at-a-time output@comma{} e.g.@comma{} when providing character echo during
keyboard input should use this command.

@example
d
@end example

This command is used to @strong{dispatch} all queued requests.  It was
renamed to a single character command (like many of the commonly used
@acronym{TTS} server commands) to work more effectively over slow
(9600) dialup lines.  The effect of calling this command is for the
@acronym{TTS} server to start processing items that have been queued
via earlier requests.

@example
s
@end example

Stop speech @strong{immediately}.  Spoken output is interrupted@comma{} and
all pending requests are flushed from the queue.

@example
q text
@end example

Queues text to be spoken. No spoken output is produced until a
@strong{dispatch} request is received via execution of command
@strong{d}.

@example
c codes
@end example

Queues synthesis codes to be sent to the @acronym{TTS} engine.  Codes
are sent to the engine with no further transformation or processing.
The codes are inserted into the output queue and will be dispatched to
the @acronym{TTS} engine at the appropriate point in the output
stream.

@example
a filename
@end example

Cues the audio file identified by filename for playing@comma{}
an @code{ogg} file.

@example
p filename
@end example

@strong{dispatch} play the audio file identified by filename for
playing  @MDash{} @code{ogg} file.

@example
t freq length
@end example

Queues a tone to be played at the specified frequency and having the
specified length.  Frequency is specified in hertz and length is
specified in milliseconds.

@example
sh duration
@end example

Queues the specified duration of silence. Silence is specified in
milliseconds.

@item
@anchor{Commands That Set State}Commands That Set State


@example
tts_reset
@end example

@strong{Immediately} reset @acronym{TTS} engine to default settings.
Stops all speech and clears the queue.

@example
tts_set_punctuations mode
@end example

Queues setting @acronym{TTS} engine to the specified punctuation
mode. Typically@comma{} @acronym{TTS} servers provide at least three modes:

@itemize
@item
None: Do not speak punctuation characters.

@item
Some: Speak some punctuation characters. Used for English prose.

@item
All: Speak out @strong{all} punctuation characters; useful in
programming modes.
@end itemize

@example
tts_set_speech_rate rate
@end example

@strong{Immediately} change speech rate. The interpretation of this
value is typically engine specific.

@example
tts_set_character_scale factor
@end example

Queues changing the scale factor.  Scale factor applied to speech rate
when speaking individual characters.Thus@comma{} setting speech rate to 500
and character scale to 1.2 will cause command @strong{l} to use a
speech rate of @strong{500 * 1.2 = 600}.

@example
tts_split_caps flag
@end example

Queues changing of the state of @strong{split caps} processing.  Turn
this on to speak mixed-case (@acronym{AKA} Camel Case) identifiers.

@example
tts_sync_state punct splitcaps caps rate
@end example

@strong{Immediately} apply the passed settings.

This ensures @strong{atomicity} i.e.@comma{} all state settings in the TTS
engine happen at one shot. Note that failure to do this might result
in some utterances being spoken with a partially set state.

@itemize
@item
punct: see tts@math{_set}@math{_punctuations}

@item
splitcaps: see tts@math{_split}@math{_caps}

@item
caps: engine specific implementation of capital letter handling

@item
rate: see tts@math{_set}@math{_speech}@math{_rate}
@end itemize

@example
set_next_lang say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the next language on the server@comma{}
maintained internally on the TTS server. If say@math{_it} is non-nil say the
language change via the TTS@.

@example
set_previous_lang say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the previous language on the server@comma{}
maintained internally on the TTS server. If say@math{_it} is non-nil say the
language change via the TTS@.

@example
set_lang language:voice say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the requested language and/or voice@comma{}
separated by a colon. Examples

@itemize
@item
set@math{_lang} "en"

@item
set@math{_lang} "en:whisper"

@item
set@math{_lang} ":whisper"
@end itemize

If say@math{_it} is non-nil@comma{} speak via the TTS the language and voice
selected.

@example
set_preferred_lang alias lang
@end example

@strong{Immediately} set an alias in the TTS server mapping for
example "en" to "en@math{_GB}". This can later be used by set@math{_lang}.
@end enumerate

@node Integration Maintenance
@section Integration Maintenance

This chapter collects source-level contracts that are deliberately omitted
from the user task guides.  Optional package APIs and renderers evolve@comma{} so
recheck the upstream interfaces and keep compatibility behavior bounded when
changing an integration.

@subsection Source checkout and byte-code lifecycle

Use the Emacs selected by the ignored repository-local @code{local.mk}.  When Emacs
31 or newer is not the system @code{emacs}@comma{} set its absolute path there:

@example
EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs
@end example

Run @code{make check-emacs} before diagnosing compilation.  After an ordinary Lisp
edit@comma{} use the incremental build:

@example
make bytecode
@end example

After switching branches@comma{} pulling changes@comma{} or changing a macro or public
interface@comma{} use:

@example
make bytecode-rebuild
@end example

That target removes ignored generated @code{.elc} files and recompiles them with
the selected Emacs; it does not clean or discard source changes.
@code{make bytecode-check} is the non-mutating preflight for startup or deployment.
It reports missing@comma{} stale@comma{} orphaned@comma{} or wrong-Emacs byte-code.

Tests deliberately prefer source and therefore do not prove that the
byte-code used by a live profile is current.  Verify rebuilt byte-code in a
fresh Emacs rather than relying on a file reload when compiled dependants may
already be resident.

During development@comma{} load the changing setup source explicitly:

@lisp
(load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")
@end lisp

The setup entry point warns and prefers source when newer Lisp would otherwise
be hidden by stale local byte-code.  In a stable checkout with current @code{.elc}
files@comma{} @code{(load-library "emacsvox-setup")} may be used.  Do not use
@code{load-library} as the first load after editing the source tree: Emacs can
select an old @code{emacsvox-setup.elc} before the newer source checker runs.

@subsection Emacsvox release staging and publication

The repository-level @code{VERSION} file is the canonical stable release
identifier.  Calendar-versioning policy@comma{} including delayed patches and
backports@comma{} is recorded in
@uref{../adr/0002-calendar-versioning.org, ADR 0002}.  When advancing a release@comma{} update @code{VERSION}@comma{} the literal
package header in @code{lisp/emacsvox.el}@comma{} and the current heading in @code{etc/NEWS}
together@comma{} then run:

@example
make version-check
@end example

Between releases@comma{} @code{etc/NEWS} uses the @code{Emacsvox Unreleased} heading@comma{} so changes
can be recorded before deciding whether the next publication is a patch or a
new feature-release series.  After publishing@comma{} preserve those notes verbatim
as @code{etc/NEWS-VERSION} and start a new unreleased file.  Ordinary
@code{version-check} accepts that state; @code{release-check}@comma{} @code{release-tag}@comma{} and
@code{release-publish} require the current NEWS heading to match @code{VERSION} exactly.

Prepare an artifact only from a separate clean@comma{} committed worktree.  Build
fresh byte-code and run the guarded artifact target:

@example
make bytecode-rebuild
make release-artifact
@end example

@code{release-artifact} runs the complete test and documentation gates before
creating @code{dist/emacsvox-YYYY.MONTH.PATCH.tar.bz2}@comma{} its SHA-256 checksum@comma{} and a
provenance record naming the checked source commit.  For a patch-zero feature
release@comma{} the series must match the current month.  A corrective or backport
release retains its original non-future series as required by ADR 0002.
Inspect the archive@comma{} checksum@comma{} and provenance before proceeding.

Stop after artifact inspection unless fresh explicit permission names the
release to be tagged and published.  Preparing an artifact@comma{} committing or
pushing a branch@comma{} publishing documentation@comma{} or receiving a general instruction
to continue is not permission to create or push a release tag.  Once that
separate authorization has been given@comma{} the guarded commands are:

@example
make release-tag
make release-publish
@end example

@code{release-tag} validates the artifact against the still-clean @code{HEAD} and creates
a local annotated@comma{} unsigned tag; it does not push the tag.  The unsigned-tag
policy and its trust boundary are recorded in
@uref{../adr/0006-annotated-unsigned-release-tags.org, ADR 0006}.  @code{release-publish}
repeats the artifact and source-commit validation@comma{} pushes only that exact tag@comma{}
and creates the GitHub release in the repository identified by
@code{RELEASE_REMOTE}.  This explicit selection prevents the GitHub CLI from
choosing an @code{upstream} remote when releasing from a fork.  No release target
pushes @code{master} or the documentation branch.  Use @code{make release-check} alone
when a non-mutating preflight is wanted without creating an artifact.

@subsection Future Aural contract decisions

The completed Aural implementation diary is preserved under
@code{archive/emacsvox/migrations/} rather than serving as current instructions.
Three questions from that diary remain deliberately undecided and require an
explicit architecture decision before implementation:

@itemize
@item
whether a critical alert may be completely suppressed or must retain an
alternative presentation;
@item
which deterministic ordering policy would apply before minor-mode selectors
can participate in the presentation cascade; and
@item
whether a new presentation occasion belongs to the core semantic contract
or to a module-owned extension.
@end itemize

Do not infer answers from the archived proposals.  Current behavior is defined
by the Aural implementation@comma{} its generated reference@comma{} and accepted ADRs.

@subsection Notmuch contracts

The live module is registered as an optional package integration.  Its stable
extension points are the Hello@comma{} Search@comma{} Show@comma{} and Tree mode hooks; Search and
Show mode maps; public interactive commands; and public Search/Show accessors.
Advice is installed only when a target exists and is target-aware@comma{} so a nested
or programmatic upstream call does not claim the outer user's feedback.

The Show landing and visibility paths also read renderer metadata such as
@code{:headers-overlay}@comma{} @code{:message-visible}@comma{} message extents@comma{} @code{:notmuch-part}@comma{}
part-hidden state@comma{} and Notmuch part-button types.  Missing optional metadata
should reduce presentation detail rather than authorize an operation.  Review
these adapters whenever the Notmuch renderer changes.

Each user-owned asynchronous Search process carries its own completion state
as a process property.  The upstream sentinel renders first; Emacsvox then
clears that property before delivering exactly one terminal result.  The
owning buffer and process status are authoritative.  A killed owner remains
silent@comma{} concurrent buffers cannot overwrite one another@comma{} and the global
refresh-all path intentionally acquires no ownership.

Run @code{make notmuch-test} for the explicit source suite.  After current byte-code
exists@comma{} run @code{make compiled-notmuch-test}; it verifies the exact loaded
@code{emacsvox-notmuch.elc}@comma{} compiler version@comma{} and the same focused selector@comma{}
including real displayed-window reading for both advance paths.

@subsection Agent Shell contracts

Use @code{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable} to reinstall speech hooks after
deliberately disabling them or while testing live integration changes.

The integration uses three deliberately different contract layers:

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .37 .45
@headitem Layer
@tab Agent Shell owner
@tab Emacsvox rule
@item Public structured API
@tab @code{agent-shell-subscribe-to}@comma{} @code{agent-shell-unsubscribe}@comma{} @code{agent-shell-status}@comma{} and @code{agent-shell-shell-buffer}; lifecycle@comma{} permission@comma{} and tool events
@tab Primary authority for processing@comma{} completion@comma{} errors@comma{} permission urgency@comma{} and tool status. The public turn boundary triggers inspection of renderer-owned response sections. Event and response data is treated as untrusted content and bounded before automatic Aural submission.
@item Experimental renderer metadata
@tab @code{agent-shell-ui-state}@comma{} @code{agent-shell-ui-section}@comma{} rendered Markdown table/source properties@comma{} and renderer-owned faces
@tab Feature-detected navigation and presentation input. Renderer group provenance outranks provider-controlled IDs; prose and IDs cannot manufacture structured permission or lifecycle authority.
@item Private compatibility fallback
@tab The aggregate @code{agent-shell--state}@comma{} the legacy viewport ownership resolver@comma{} viewport position helpers@comma{} and legacy face@comma{} prompt@comma{} or ID inference
@tab Kept in narrow adapter functions for older Agent Shell releases or incomplete renderers. A missing optional detail may reduce header or navigation richness but must not create a permission action or completed response.
@end multitable

The lifecycle subscription currently consumes @code{init-started}@comma{}
@code{init-finished}@comma{} @code{session-selected}@comma{} @code{session-restored}@comma{} @code{input-submitted}@comma{}
@code{turn-complete}@comma{} and @code{error}.  Dedicated subscriptions consume
@code{permission-request}@comma{} @code{permission-response}@comma{} and @code{tool-call-update}.  Add new
semantic behavior at the public event layer when Agent Shell exposes it; keep
renderer and private fallbacks isolated and covered by compatibility tests.

@subsection WSL2 Omnivox development staging

The ordinary WSL2 user route is implemented by
@code{bin/emacsvox-wsl-install} and pinned by @code{etc/wsl-install.conf}.  Treat either
file as release tooling.  When advancing GNU Emacs or Omnivox@comma{} update the
version@comma{} archive name@comma{} URL@comma{} and SHA-256 together; exercise x64 and ARM64
selection in the isolated installer tests; and perform one real installation
of the newly pinned archive before updating the user manual.  The installer
must remain per-user@comma{} must not invoke @code{sudo} or edit shell startup files@comma{} and
must preserve an explicit @code{EMACS} or @code{local.mk} selection rather than silently
replacing it.

The installed Omnivox executable is recorded as an absolute WSL path in the
data-only @code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox-program} file.  Launcher selection
is explicit @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM}@comma{} that per-user file@comma{} developer staging@comma{} then
@code{PATH}.  Keep the selection tests synchronized with this order.  A configured
but invalid path is a visible configuration error; silently falling through
would hide a stale or damaged user installation.

This section describes the reproducible source-build path@comma{} not the ordinary
pre-built WSL2 installation.  The complete source-build prerequisite checklist
is in
@ref{Building Omnivox For WSL2}.  Dependency ownership is deliberately
split across three boundaries:

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .30 .52
@headitem Boundary
@tab Supplied by
@tab Dependencies
@item Windows and WSL host
@tab Maintainer
@tab WSL interoperation@comma{} @code{powershell.exe}@comma{} @code{wslpath}@comma{} Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or newer@comma{} Docker with Linux/AMD64 support@comma{} Git@comma{} Make@comma{} download and checksum tools@comma{} standard GNU shell utilities@comma{} network access@comma{} and writable Windows @code{LocalAppData}.
@item Pinned release toolchain
@tab Emacsvox target
@tab The immutable Rust/Debian container@comma{} MinGW cross tools and runtime DLLs@comma{} CMake@comma{} Clang and libclang@comma{} eSpeak build dependencies@comma{} Roslyn@comma{} .NET 4.0 reference assemblies@comma{} and official Windows Piper companion.  The exact versions@comma{} source commit@comma{} and downloaded-package hashes live in @code{servers/omnivox-release/toolchain.lock}.
@item Build source
@tab Git checkouts
@tab The current Emacsvox checkout and the Omnivox checkout selected by @code{OMNIVOX_DIR}@comma{} including Omnivox's locked Rust dependencies and checksum-locked Flite and RuTTS source inputs.
@end multitable

@code{make verify-windows-omnivox-toolchain} prepares the cached Roslyn inputs@comma{}
builds the Docker image@comma{} and validates this boundary before compilation.  The
full target then builds Omnivox's two 32-bit helper sources deterministically@comma{}
builds native Windows Omnivox plus its RHVoice@comma{} Flite@comma{} and RuTTS helpers in the
container@comma{} and stages their licenses@comma{} provenance@comma{} and checksums.  The clean
target also builds Piper support and verifies that the pinned companion and
main executable identify the same Omnivox source commit.  A host Rust
installation@comma{} MinGW installation@comma{} Visual Studio@comma{} .NET SDK@comma{} or system eSpeak
installation is not an input to this release path.  RHVoice's native library@comma{}
languages@comma{} and voices remain external user-supplied runtime inputs.  Flite's
CMU US SLT voice is compiled into its checksum-locked helper.  RuTTS's built-in
male and female Russian voices are compiled from the checksum-locked 6.3.3
source; RuLex is not included.

After staging@comma{} the static verifier rechecks manifests@comma{} provenance@comma{} the
Windows-local copy@comma{} and all configured external inputs.  The separate live
verifier executes that exact Windows-local runtime@comma{} prewarms the bounded eSpeak
voice cache tied to the verified content-addressed data@comma{} discovers the compiled
Flite and RuTTS voices@comma{} and requires real WAV synthesis from both helpers.
Use @code{make verify-windows-omnivox-live} to repeat only the executable smoke gate.

@code{OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL} is an optional staging input@comma{} not a release payload.
When supplied@comma{} the target validates the adjacent configuration@comma{} copies both
files into a content-addressed Windows-local model cache@comma{} records their hashes@comma{}
and writes the Windows path used by the launcher.  The recursive runtime
manifest deliberately excludes model files.  A different model therefore
creates a distinct local build identity without changing the pinned companion.
The public-domain engineering voice used in Omnivox CI is approved only for
that bounded test purpose and is not a model-distribution default.

Maintainers testing tracked Emacsvox or Omnivox changes may use
@code{make windows-omnivox-dev}.  That target records both worktree-diff hashes and
labels the result as a development build.  It builds the RHVoice@comma{} Flite@comma{} and
RuTTS companions but omits Piper@comma{} because the currently pinned Piper companion
must not be mixed with later Omnivox source.  It also rejects
@code{OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL}.  Advance the checked Piper companion and source commit
together before these integrations enter the clean release path.  See
@code{servers/omnivox-release/README.org} for the pinned toolchain@comma{} licensing@comma{}
provenance@comma{} and restaging contract.

The development target accepts @code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_LIBRARY} and
@code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_DATA} together@comma{} plus an optional
@code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_CONFIG} directory.  It resolves WSL or Windows absolute paths@comma{}
requires the C API DLL and the language/voice data layout@comma{} records content
digests in provenance and machine-local Windows path pointers beside the
selected runtime@comma{} and leaves all RHVoice files external.  Those pointers let
the launcher restore the variables for every fresh Emacsvox process.  The
runtime verifier rejects missing or changed external inputs.  Omitting all
three variables leaves RHVoice unconfigured without affecting Flite@comma{} RuTTS@comma{}
or the other engines.  RuTTS requires no external installation; the staged
helper always provides its built-in male and female Russian voices without
RuLex.

Do not bypass the Omnivox release target manually or clean either repository
to make its release guard pass.  Use @code{make windows-omnivox} only for the
reproducible clean-release path documented in the user-facing WSL2 route.
